Q: How to get around JSON parsers refusing to parse a “new Date(…)” call? A: <p>Like others have pointed out, it's invalid JSON.  One solution is to use <code>eval()</code> instead of <code>JSON.parse()</code> but that leaves you with a potential security issue instead.</p>

<p>A better approach might be to search for and replace these offending issues, turning the data into valid JSON:</p>

<pre><code>fullResultJSON = fullResultJSON.replace(/new Date\((\d+)\)/g, '$1');
</code></pre>

<p>You can even go one step further and "revive" these fields into JavaScript Date objects using the second argument for <code>JSON.parse()</code>:</p>

<pre><code>var messageAsJSObj = JSON.parse(fullResultJSON, function (key, value) {
    if (key == "DateTime")
        return new Date(value);

    return value;
}); 
</code></pre>

<p>Here's an example: <a href="http://jsfiddle.net/AndyE/vcXnE/" rel="nofollow">http://jsfiddle.net/AndyE/vcXnE/</a></p>

