Q: How to attach a PDF file on WhatsApp Web using Selenium? (Error: “The file you tried to add is not supported”) A: <ol>
<li><p>Yes WhatsApp has limitations, for example you can only send files smaller then 16mb, but thats not a limitation due to selenium. Hower it's the only one I encounterd so far.</p>
</li>
<li><p>The selector seems to be right. I also tried this way, but it left me with a open file dialog, that I was not able to close, so I for me decided that this is not the way I would go.</p>
</li>
<li><p>I would not say that the way is recommended, but there are other ways to upload files to WhatsApp. You can drag and drop files into the chat area or you could paste them into the message box. To drag and drop looks to be more complicated so I think the easiest way to handle this is to paste them into the message box. My solution to this is the following:</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>Inspired by the answers of <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/70915646">Mahmudur Rahman Shovon</a> and <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/59559554">pguardiario</a> to somewhat related questions I found the following works for me.</p>
<p>Assuming you allready instantiated your driver with <code>driver = webdriver.Chrome()</code>, set a wait with <code>wait = WebDriverWait(driver, 60)</code> and you store the absoulute path of the file you want to send somewhere you could do the following:</p>
<pre><code>def paste_file(self, absolute_path, driver, wait):
    JAVA_SCRIPT = &quot;&quot;&quot;
        const messageField = arguments[0];
        const type = arguments[1];
        const content = arguments[2];
        const name = arguments[3];
        const response = await fetch(`data:${type};base64,${content}`);
        const blob = await response.blob();
        const file = new File([blob], name, {type: blob.type});
        const dataTransfer = new DataTransfer();
        dataTransfer.items.add(file);
        const event = new ClipboardEvent('paste', {
            clipboardData: dataTransfer,
            bubbles: true
        });
        messageField.dispatchEvent(event);
    &quot;&quot;&quot;
    xpath = &quot;//*[@data-testid='conversation-compose-box-input']&quot;
    message_field = wait.until(EC.element_to_be_clickable((By.XPATH, xpath)))
    mimeType, encoding = mimetypes.guess_type(absolute_path)
    content = base64.b64encode(open(absolute_path, 'rb').read()).decode('utf8')
    name = os.path.basename(absolute_path)
    driver.execute_script(JAVA_SCRIPT, message_field, mimeType, content, name)
</code></pre>
<p>This method then pastes the file into the chat message box and whatsapp decides how to display it depending on the type of the file. If you want you can checkout <a href="https://github.com/Markus-Teichmann/WA-Selenium/tree/main" rel="nofollow noreferrer">my github project</a>, where I use a simmilar method in the driver class. I did not write a README till now so it is emty but the code works ^^ (at least until xpaths break...)</p>

Q: iOS: AVPlayer - getting a snapshot of the current frame of a video A: <p>Swift version of <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/users/22147/gordon-childs">Gordon Childs</a>' solution which still works as expected in Xcode 15 and iOS 17:</p>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>private var playerOutput: AVPlayerItemVideoOutput?
(...)
self.playerOutput = AVPlayerItemVideoOutput(pixelBufferAttributes: [kCVPixelBufferPixelFormatTypeKey as String: Int(kCVPixelFormatType_32BGRA)])
(...)
self.playerItem?.add(self.playerOutput!)
(...)
if let playerItem = self?.playerItem, let imageBuffer = self?.playerOutput?.copyPixelBuffer(forItemTime: playerItem.currentTime(), itemTimeForDisplay: nil), let image = UIImage(pixelBuffer: imageBuffer) {
  // do what you want with the `image`
} else {
  print(&quot;Failed to grab frame&quot;)
}
</code></pre>
